{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549482-bastet-mechanical-deep-dive","id":1549482,"title":"Bastet (Mechanical Deep Dive)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-03-21T09:54:50-07:00","id":1549482,"title":"Bastet (Mechanical Deep Dive)","content":"Alright. Now this thread might actually be quite long!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:28px\"><i><b>Werecats!</b></i></span> The Bastet claim to be the youngest Changing Breed, since Cat is a very selective type and Gaia had to come to him and ask for help before he would create a Changing Breed with her. Bastet lore holds that Gaia created them to rule over the Gaian Shifters.<br /> <br /> Divided into species-based Tribes, the Bastet are about as diverse as the Garou, but far rarer due to the losses suffered during the War of Rage. Still, by Fera standards, they're not all that rare, especially in Africa, South America and Asia.<br /> <br /> Naturally this thread is not a substitute for W20 Changing Breeds; I won't be getting as much into the lore, geography, character and stuff, mostly the mechanics.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Yava</b></u><br /> <br /> The Yava are supernatural weaknesses of certain Fera, the Bastet and the Ajaba. If you know all of the Yava a group suffers under then you know how to exploit their weaknesses to the utmost; supernatural abilities against a target whose Yava you know are at -2 difficulty, and using supernatural abilities against someone who knows your Yava gains a +2 difficulty.<br /> <br /> So, for example, an Ajaba trying to use a Gift such as Pulse of the Prey against a Simba would most likely suffer +2 difficulty. Meanwhile a Simba would very likely have a -2 difficulty to use the same Gift against an Ajaba, as most of them have been taught the Yava of the Ajaba. The Yava of the various Tribes will be covered below.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><u><b>Tribes</b></u></span><br /> <br /> As I review the Tribes I will be giving them a Red, Amber or Green rating for Yava, Forms, Stats and Gifts respectively. These ratings aren't meant to put you off playing them, but something to keep in mind when considering your character, especially if you enjoy some gentle character-optimization. If a Tribe has a Red rating for Yava it probably means there's an insta-kill in there, which might be worth avoiding with the relevant Merit. Stats-wise it's beneficial if you have the option of playing low-rage, and if the total number of your Rage + Willpower is good. For example, Rage 1 Willpower 5 would beat Rage 5 Willpower 1; they add up to the same value, but the former has the option of being Rage 1, while the latter must be Rage 5.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that a Tribe with amazing Forms, stats and Gifts, and a generous set of Yava, could be contextually worse than another Tribe in your story, or make for a worse character. When I rate these aspects I'm very much boiling them down to the bare essentials.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Bagheera</u></b><br /> Wereleopards are noted to be mystics, though they share this with many Tribes.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Their Crinos form gives +3/2/3 while their Chatro form gives +2/3/3<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 2, Willpower 4.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Their Yava are sleeping during the new moon (though they can be awoken by violence), being forced to follow a trail of salt if they see one, and that if their prey are blessed with a special sign of protection they will be forced to flee if their rage is not just.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> Bagheera get a few interesting unique Gifts, including a meditation stat boost, a time-slowing debuff and a mystical superform.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Balam</b></u><br /> Werejaguars are Rage-filled and have relative expertise in uncovering lost and foreign Gifts and Rites, and have even been known to learn the powers of Fetishes for themselves.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Both Crinos and Chatro forms give +3/2/3<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 4, Willpower 3.<br /> Having a total of 7 is good, but you are forced into a high-Rage character here.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Demons will force you into a maddening rage, burning your heart (presumably after your death) will permanently destroy your soul, and a means of instantly killing you by taking the feathers shed by the Tribe's flight gift and burning them in your face.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> True Fear, Wings of the Pegasus, this list is pretty decent. Their Level 5 Gift, Feed the Gods, has the potential to instantly kill a target by force-pulling their heart out, but the odds of this actually happening are very slim.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Bubasti</b></u><br /> Shifters who are kin to the Kyphur cats of Egypt, the Bubasti are considered beautiful and mysterious even by the standards of the Bastet. For the third time in a row we have a Tribe of magicians, but the Bubasti are said to be even EVEN more good at it. This includes copying foreign Gifts and learning 'cat magic' spells, though anyone of Rank 2+ who practices such sorcery has a light scent of the Wyrm about them.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +1/3/1, Chatro gives +2/3/1<br /> Mechanically these forms aren't amazing, but your Feline form blends in decently well; you look like a rare variety of cat, not a wild animal.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 1, Willpower 5.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> 2/3 of these are related to the Tribe as a whole, making you relatively light on personal weaknesses, though the politics of your Yava will still bind you quite badly. If all of the Immortals of the Bubasti die, the whole Tribe dies. If there isn't a single Bubasti in the land of Khem then the Tribe becomes doomed. Finally, on a personal note, you will eat any food put before you.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> Bubasti Gifts include magical charity, perfect translation of written language, and an annoyingly weak Bane-banishing Gift with severe downsides. Their list is very cool, but it is a little lacking in mystic powerhouses... until you get to the Rank 2 Spirit Ward, normally unlocked at Rank 4, which is amazing.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Ceilican</b></u><br /> Fae-touched and able to change their Pyrio (Auspice) as they go through life, Ceilican can constantly reinvent themselves, and have even changed species to evade death!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +1/3/1, Chatro gives +0/4/1<br /> +4 Dexterity is great, but the lack of a significant Strength and Stamina buff is a little worrying.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 3, Willpower 3.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Ceilican suffer from pure iron as if it were silver, speaking their birth name backwards makes them uncomfortable, and once per year they must completely abandon their memories and identity and start over anew. The final Yava is very problematic but could be a great plot point in a game with a ST who is ready to do something interesting with it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> These include musical instrument mastery, enchanting weapons, summoning Fae allies and turning into a housecat. All in all I would say that Faerie Kin alone makes this list Green, so long as the Storyteller is ready to roll with the Changeling angle, and if they aren't, you probably shouldn't be playing a Ceilican.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Khan</b></u><br /> Powerful and filled with Rage, Khan are closer to Garou than other Bastet and are primarily focused on raw power.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +3/2/3, Chatro gives +4/2/3<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 5, Willpower 2.<br /> Having a total of 7 is good, and if you're playing a Khan, chances are you were always going to go Rage 5. Context makes this Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Fall asleep during a solar eclipse. If you are presented with the meat of a human child you will be forced to eat it. A direct challenge can't be ignored or you lose your Rage. I'm marking this as Red because the 'refuse a challenge' aspect is a real weakness which will come up almost every session; it's a great weakness to have, it's great storytelling material, but it's dangerous. Khan are powerful and extreme, and this weakness forces you to keep that in mind!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> Bonus soak, attack spirits through the gauntlet, walk with the weight of a petal, a very powerful dragon attack, and a neat shockwave. This is a very solid combat list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-22-2025, 05:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-21T10:46:11-07:00","id":1549483,"title":"","content":"Bubasti forms might not be buff stat-wise, but they appear to be servals and caracals in Feline, so you have a much lower chance of someone calling in a severe law enforcement response, and some people might even just let you be on account of IT'S A KITTY! even without using the Gift, especially if savannah cats are common where you are. On the flipside, you might become an unintentional internet celebrity...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-22T02:01:51-07:00","id":1549496,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Pumonca</b></u><br /> Werecougars wander North America and are heavily disparate, more likely to meet other Fera than their own kind. Being the main North American Bastet also means this is probably the most realistic Bastet to play in a random game set in the USA.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +2/3/3, Chatro gives +3/3/2<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 4, Willpower 4.<br /> The first 8! This is undoubtedly amazing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Leaving 'your land' for more than a moon cycle kills you, being immersed in poisons which harm the land kills you, every animal except the 'great cats' fears you and flees from you.<br /> <br /> So, what we have here is a guy who might be stuck in a tristate area (works fine for most games), at most leaving it for a week or two. If you get immersed in a tank of toxic sewage you die, no questions asked... and you're going to set off every dog, cat, cow, bird, wolf, fox and so on you encounter. I'd strongly recommend using the relevant Merit to ignore the second Yava in the list here, and perhaps to overcome all three for 5 Freebies.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> Pumonca can mimic any voice or sound they've heard in their life, like a Corax, and they also get Call Elemental.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Qualmi</b></u><br /> Introspective and wise, the Qualmi are meant to remind other Shifters of their duties. Sounds like a really dangerous job, especially where Garou are concerned; people love being told how to do their job! They're lynxes, for what it's worth.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +1/2/2, Chatro gives +2/3/2<br /> Using Chatro makes this okay - it's slightly worse than average but not terrible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 2, Willpower 5.<br /> Total of 7, and the choice to leave Rage low if you prefer. At Rage 2 you'll only be bothering exceptionally weak-willed humans with your Curse.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Unraveling your riddles undoes your magic, pollution poisoning your local salmon will harm you, you're unable to tell people about your parents and if you see proof of who they were you become confused 'for days'.<br /> <br /> The riddles one is a little confusing, but a good Storyteller can do interesting things with it. Needing to ensure your local salmon are looked after sounds like a faff, but could be interesting. The parents one is unlikely to come up, but someone being able to confuse you 'for days' is a potentially severe weakness.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> Camouflage while naked (i.e. in any form besides Homid), +3 to Enigmas, a powerful Enigmas-based Gift where you terrify a group of people by confusing their sense of direction, potentially triggering a fox frenzy or total panic, and an xray vision power with a huge range.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Simba</b></u><br /> My impression of the Simba isn't great, lore-wise, but their mechanics are pretty great. The Simba see themselves as the natural rulers of the Bastet and indeed of all Fera, a bit like Silver Fangs but for the whole of Gaia.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +3/2/3, Chatro gives +4/2/3<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 5, Willpower 2.<br /> Total of 7, very Rage-heavy, but if you're playing a Simba you were probably going to grab Rage 5.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> A rope made of your mane will hold you, &quot;stealing your roar&quot; will put you in a very difficult position and force you not to harm the one who has it, and if you're male, you'll never let someone kill your wife (husband of the year), and you can't kill your wives either.<br /> <br /> Female Simba basically have 2 Yava instead of 3, which is quite funny. The rope made of your mane thing is great, very folklore-y. Stealing your roar is given no context, so presumably magic is required. The wives one is more the area of NPCs.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> +1 to Intimidation and Leadership, force others to kneel, Luna's Armour, Coup de Grace! This list is very good, with a mixture of leadership and combat focus.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Swara</b></u><br /> Fast-moving werecheetahs, Swara love fixing problems but they are also particularly xenophobic, meaning that a Swara who bonds with outsiders will be an exception.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Forms</b></span><br /> Crinos gives +2/3/2, Chatro gives +2/4/2.<br /> +4 Dexterity is very good, especially with +2 Strength and +2 Stamina. That is all.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Stats</b></span><br /> Rage 2, Willpower 4.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Yava</b></span><br /> Insulting your mother (figuratively as well as literally) drives you mad, soil from THE MOON drugs you if you drink it, and you fear Frenzy so you seek to avoid it at all costs.<br /> <br /> Being very wary of Frenzy is wise, but the Yava suggests that the Swara have especially good reason to fear Frenzy, as it lets the Wyrm in. The mother one is worse than you might imagine, someone calling you a &quot;son-of-a-bitch&quot; might provoke a Rage roll or force you to punch them in the face.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gifts</b></span><br /> Step Sideways at Rank 2. Beyond that... a bunch of mediocre stuff stolen from the Silent Striders. On the assumption that you don't just grab Step Sideways, which is meant to be a very rare Merit and is very expensive anyways, being able to Step Sideways at Rank 2 (likely most of the game's runtime) rather than Rank 4 is a huge improvement. I wish this list had more to it, but you know.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-22T03:59:12-07:00","id":1549498,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\"><u><b>Breeds</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Bastet are divided into Homid, Metis and Feline. They don't use the Garou Breed Gifts however, unlike the Ajaba they have their own Breed Gifts. Metis are still deformed but face no discrimination from the Bastet.<br /> <br /> Flavour-wise I'm very glad that Bastet get their own Breed Gifts, but they really suffer from it in a way. The Garou Breed lists are immense. These are very short. That said, as a Bastet you have a very good opportunity to steal Gifts from other Shapeshifters, so perhaps consider these lists a starting point.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Homid</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Gnosis 1</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Urban Hunter (City Running)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sweet Hunter's Smile (Persuasion)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Eavesdropper's Ear (Whisper Catching)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Jam Technology</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Cowing the Bullet</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Craft of the Maker (Reshape Object)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Monkey's Uncle (Doppleganger)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Tech Speak</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Madness</b></span><br /> <br /> Monkey's Uncle is slightly changed from Doppleganger, as a Bastet using Monkey's Uncle can disguise themselves as a human, Bastet or Great Cat, rather than just humans.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Cat Claws (1):</b></u></span> Get the partial transformation of Crinos claws in your human-like forms without spending Willpower, and the difficulty of the transformation is reduced to 6. What this means, essentially, is that you can get high-damage Aggravated claws in your Homid and Sokto (akin to Glabro) forms. This is actually pretty useful! It will also work well with any claw-buffing Gifts.<br /> <br /> This list suffers a little from lacking Apecraft's Blessings and stealing some of the worst Homid Gifts, like Cowing and Reshape.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Metis</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Gnosis 3</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Create Element</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Shed</b></span><br /> (No pre-existing Level 2 Gifts)<br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Visceral Agony</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Moon's Gateway (Open Moon Bridge)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Wrath of Nala (Divine Wind)</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Primal Nature (1):</b></u></span> This is Sense Wyrm on steroids. For a +1 difficulty modifier you get to sense each member of the Triat, distinguishing their energies from one another. So you might use this Gift and notice that a person is heavily tainted by both Weaver and Wyrm, which immediately gives you a lot of clues as to their true nature.<br /> <br /> I have a feeling that if the Garou had this Gift on their Metis, the World of Darkness would probably be in better shape!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Blinding Moonbeam Gaze (2):</b></u></span> Your eyes become like headlights on a car, which is very funny to me. Roll Gnosis, difficulty 7. For the scene you may turn the lights on and off at will. They illuminate darkness, which is handy, and also &quot;distract&quot; and &quot;blind&quot; people, but we are given no mechanics for this.<br /> <br /> Given that Bastet get night vision in their General list, this isn't great.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Whisker Sight (2):</b></u></span> Roll Perception + Primal-Urge (difficulity 7). Success grants you the power to see everything within arm's reach of your body, even if it's invisible or shrouded, out of sight, and so on. The utility of this is pretty great; you will notice a lot of stuff moving around a scene slowly with this turned on. &quot;Everything within arm's reach&quot; is a pretty big distance in Crinos and Chatro too! Great for combat, detective work, avoiding being ambushed and so on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Fist of Cahlash (3):</b></u></span> Snarl at something, rolling Rage (difficulty 6 for objects, difficulty 8 for characters). Every success turns 10 pounds of matter to dust, or inflicts a level of Bashing damage. Examples used for object destruction are weakening a building or destroying toxic waste. The ability to clean up the world is pretty nice. You do risk Frenzying using this Gift however, if you have high Rage!<br /> <br /> This Gift gets Green because you can use it on anything you can see and which can &quot;hear&quot; you. The briefcase the pentex rep is running away with. The rungs of that ladder. The key in the Fomor's hand. The jeweled sceptre a dickish Simba says makes him the rightful ruler of all of Gaia's children. And so on. No protections for Fetishes and other special objects are mentioned!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Feline</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gnosis 5</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Heightened Senses</b></span>, <b><span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Pounce (Hare's Leap)</span></b><br /> <i><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Catfeet</b></span></i>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Prehensile Tail (Monkey Tail)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Chains of Mist</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Underbelly (Fatal Flaw)</b></span><br /> <b>Beast Life</b><br /> <b>Revolt of the Land (Gaia's Vengeance)</b><br /> <br /> Sadly Feline Bastet get two unrated Gifts (see their relevant entries), a Red, and a bunch of Ambers. But the Greens are great... and they'd get them immediately with the Lupus list. Catfeet is confusingly included here, despite being a Level 1 Gift in the General list. Though if you take the RAW extremely seriously you could ask to buy it a second time and double up on the difficulty reduction <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mine (1):</b></u></span> Rub yourself against an object and spend 1 Gnosis. Anyone trying to steal the object from you has to pass a Willpower roll (difficulty 9) before they can do so.<br /> <br /> This is great and very flavourful! Use this on your Fetishes, your valuables, stuff you steal, you name it. The duration is permanent and the cost is very doable. Great for a conquerer and for a cat burglar, hell, great for a Player Character since even in a very grounded game they tend to be slightly more looty than an average NPC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-23-2025, 04:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-22T18:58:21-07:00","id":1549506,"title":"","content":"Catfeet is extraneously in the Feline list at a higher rank when it's a starting Gift for any Bastet, so I'm calling situationally red on that one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-23T04:31:53-07:00","id":1549521,"title":"","content":"<u><b>General Bastet Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Worth noting that Bastet are prone to learning Gifts from eachother, and to stealing them from other Shifters. These Gifts are a flavour of the abilities most Bastet are able to most easily learn.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Catfeet</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Diamond Claws (Razor Claws)</b></span>, <b><span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Lick Wounds (Mother's Touch)</span></b>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Open Seal</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense Magic</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense the Truth (Truth of Gaia)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense Unmaker's Hand (Sense Wyrm)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Cat Sight (Eyes of the Cat)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Eerie Eyes (Staredown)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>First Slash (Spirit of the Fray)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Pulse of the Prey</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Sense Silver</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Swipe (Taking the Forgotten)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Farsight (Scrying)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Invisibility</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Touch the Mind (Mental Speech)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Wolf's Terror (Silver Claws)</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Banish Sickness (1):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Medicine (difficulty depending on severity of the illness), and you need to lick your target. On a success you cure the target's disease, so long as it is curable.<br /> <br /> This is the missing piece that makes a great healer. Typical Garou healers are great at keeping you alive, restoring health levels will even stop someone from dying of poison or acute illness, but curing the disease is often beyond them and requires a Talen or Ritual or Spirit. This skips all that. It's a great bargaining chip which can ingratiate you with various NPCs, Veil and local culture permitting.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Silent Stalking (1):</b></u></span> Dexterity + Stealth difficulty 5, on a success you can move silently over ANY surface (fallen autumn leaves, creaking floorboards) for the scene. No cost, just a risk of the Gift being blocked off if you fail the test. More Gifts should have this stipulation about failure to avoid pointless rerolls.<br /> <br /> A solid stealth buff which is well worth 3xp.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Night's Passage (2):</b></u></span> So long as you are in shadows, even if the shadows are mostly illuminated by sudden bright light but still exist, you remain invisible. Dexterity + Subterfuge/Occult, difficulty 7, remain invisible until you are out of a shadow or attack someone, and the cost is 1 Gnosis.<br /> <br /> This is a very solid invisibility power which would be usable in most rooms under most circumstances, and in many outdoor environments. Just remember that invisible is not undetectable. Scent of Running Water would complement this further, as would Blur of the Milky Eye (which effects all attempts to find you, not just visual, based on wording).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Shriek (2):</b></u></span> Stamina + Intimidation (difficulty 7), on a success everyone within a 20ft range is deafened for 1 turn per success. Great ability, no cost, you can use it any time, deafening a crowd of enemies and then slipping into the shadows invisible would be a very good combination for instance. Those deafened also get a +1 difficulty modifier while the effect is ongoing!<br /> <br /> If used strategically you could use this against a crowd of enemies before your allies step sideways or drop down from above, that sort of thing. The lack of selectiveness isn't ideal, but the absoluteness of the wording is great. I wouldn't say that protective earpieces would help against this Gift, and I believe it would work on spirits and creatures that hear but normally couldn't be deafened (hearing with hair all over their skin etc.).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Caper (3):</b></u></span> Very powerful distraction power. This Gift requires you to move or perform in a way which draws the eyes of bystanders, for example a beautiful dance in homid or gracefully skulking around in your feline form. A difficulty 3 Manipulation + Expression guarantees a lot of successes, and so long as you have more successes than the WP of bystanders they're mystically forced to pay attention to you. Violence breaks the spell, and you have to keep performing, but your allies could get up to a bunch of useful things in the background.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Impala's Flight (3):</b></u></span> Stamina + Athletics difficulty 6, for 2 turns per success you double your running speed. No cost, and you can use this as often as you like.<br /> <br /> Given that your feline form may be very, very fast indeed (anywhere from 40-120km/h), the ability to double that speed might be useful. The Gift being free is neat, but I'd like it if you could make it last for a scene by spending a point of Rage or similar. All in all this is pretty good, especially in chase scenes and giant maps.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Clawstorm (4):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Rage and 1 Willpower, make 4 melee-range attacks in your turn. This seems to mean that you do all of these attacks during YOUR turn, rather than the 'extra' attacks going at the end of the round as they typically would with Rage.<br /> <br /> Naturally you don't get to spend Rage for bonus attacks if you use this Gift... but you can use Rage for bonus ACTIONS if you use this Gift. So, for example, you could spend 1R1W for 4 attacks on your turn, and 2R for +2 actions (dodges, running away, environmental checks) in the same round, assuming a permanent Rage of 5.<br /> <br /> You're limited to (Homid Stamina) uses of this Gift each Scene, so for example with a Stamina of 4 you could use this Gift 4 times in a single battle. This is a great saving in terms of Rage and the turn economy of it is great, since you get your 4 attacks upfront; with spirit of the fray you could very easily kill many Ahroun with this. Because what tends to happen is that two Rage-heavy characters both do a single attack, both are fine, and it's the bonus attacks at the end of the round which decide who lives and who dies. With this Gift you can kill your foe before they have a chance to do a single Rage action, even if they're above you in initiative.<br /> <br /> This is also a great boon to powerful Bastet with a Rage of 1-4, who normally wouldn't be doing so many bonus attacks anyways. It also saves on Rage vs how much you'd normally need to spend to do this many attacks.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Walking Between Worlds (4):</b></u></span> Allows you to step sideways. Taking the Merit, or a Fetish, to do so sooner wouldn't be unwise. That said a huge part of why Bastet have such incredible Gifts is that they are missing the ability to enter and leave the Umbra at will. So in a typical game you'll be waiting until Rank 4 to get this... though I could see it causing problems for the flow of the game if you are the only person in the party who can't step sideways.<br /> <br /> So this is Green if you need it, and irrelevant if you don't.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Perfect Passage (5):</b></u></span> Become intangible to objects, but remain tangible to people. This is a bit of a complex intangibility power since it has caveats; if you don't see a knife or bullet coming it can hit you, so you need to be aware of something to let it pass through you harmlessly. But this Gift lets you move through non-living weapons, walls, armour and so on. You're also immune to things like poison gases, but you are still breathing.<br /> <br /> You can pick up an object and it becomes tangible to you in this form. Your clothing and held objects are along for the ride, letting you do weird things like pull a chain through a wall.<br /> <br /> 1 Gnosis, Wits + Larceny (difficulty 6), lasts for 5 minutes per success. A practically legendary power. This is especially useful against foes who depend on Melee and Firearms, along with explosives and so-on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Withering Stare (5):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Rage (difficulty of the target's Willpower). Every success inflicts a level of Aggravated damage. We've seen a lot of iterations of this Gift, and it isn't that great for Rank 5, unless you need to kill a weakling with a glance, which is potentially very intimidating to bystanders.<br /> <br /> The problem is that rolling Rage against a difficulty of 3, say with a weak enemy, is going to put you into a Frenzy or Thrall. This Gift can only EVER kill a target if you get 7+ successes after all! It doesn't feel like that's been thought out. <hr /><br /> This is a GIANT general list. Sense Magic! Mother's Touch! Sense Wyrm as standard, and Metis can get an even better version to go with it, you could get either or both. Catfeet at Level 1, making it being Level 2 for Felines redundant. Razor Claws is a favourite, and Truth of Gaia is a standout. Essentially this list feels like it was put together by a Bastet player, which makes me smile. Who needs a good Tribal or Breed list with Gifts like these? Though having other good lists won't hurt at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-23T13:18:13-07:00","id":1549525,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Beast Courts</b></u><br /> <br /> Bastet are welcome in the Beast Courts, and thus can gain access to the Court Gifts. These Gifts are covered here:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549277#post1549277\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549277#post1549277\"> Garou Gifts: Ranking, Thinking, Overthinking - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549277#post1549277\"> https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549277#post1549277 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> I'm going to break down Gifts by level and category, bit by bit. If it's Wyrmish, Fera-only, obscure or lost it's going to feature much much later, if at all. This is intended to focus on the Gifts you will actually be getting in play, hence starting at level 1 and working up. I'll be considering utility (both inside and </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Part the Wall</b></u></span> at Rank 3 is a real upgrade for most Bastet, who typically get to Step Sideways at Rank 4. <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Lantern </b></span>is a great buff to most Bastet, as it grants Willpower generation and various powerful social Gifts. <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Fists</b></span> gaining Resist Pain is a neat benefit, and Stoking Fury's Furnace is always a good addition. <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Mirror</b></span> is a brilliant addition to almost any Werecat since it grants Spirit Speech, Persuasion, Command Spirit and various other Umbra powers. <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Leaf </b></span>is likely the worst; Bastet get many of these Gifts already. Since Bastet get healing by default, there isn't much point to <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Pillar</b></span>. <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Courtier </b></span>getting Persuasion, Flame Dance, Scent of the Oathbreaker and a few other benefits makes it quite worthwhile.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Bagheera Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Treeclimber</span> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">(Balance)</span></b><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Lawgiver's Legacy (Command the Gathering)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Eye of the Cobra</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Weak Arm</b></span><br /> <br /> Treeclimber is rated Red because Catfeet, available at Level 1 in the General List for Bastet, makes it essentially redundant.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Humbaba's Escape (1):</b></u></span> -2 difficulty to rolls to escape restraints and squeeze through small spaces. This is entirely passive, which I like, and of course for 3xp this is probably just about worth it, but if I'm getting a Gift to help with escapology I expect it to be like &quot;you can fit through any gap bigger than a grape&quot; not &quot;-2 difficulty to a roll you'll almost never make&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ojas Surge (2):</b></u></span> Meditate for (6 - Rank) minutes, roll Gnosis (difficulty 7), and boost a variety of Attributes with the successes. The bonus can boost a stat above 5.<br /> <br /> A highly niche use of this Gift is that it overcomes a problem WTA characters can face at very high levels. Bonus actions are limited by Dexterity and Wits. Say you have Rage 9, Dexterity 5 and Wits 5. You can only take 5 actions each turn, meaning that you can't spend 5 Rage to take 5 bonus actions for a total of 6. If, however, your Dexterity were boosted by your fighting form to 6 or 7, and your Wits were boosted by Ojas Surge to 6, you'd be able to manage it. A more likely problem to overcome would be if you had enough Rage to take 4 actions in a round (Rage 5) but you had a Wits below 4; this Gift could help alleviate that problem... if you can meditate for a few minutes prior to battle. Not impossible! But niche.<br /> <br /> Beyond that it's handy to boost Stamina for Soak, Dexterity for dodge/hits/damage, Strength for feats of strength and damage, and various Attributes for various different Gift activations. The duration is a single scene, but this could be great for projects, crafting and so on, or preparing for a Rite. The lack of cost is also very neat. If you have time to prep there's never any reason not to use this.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>The Paradox of Time (4):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Enigmas (difficulty of target's Willpower), and for each success the target is disoriented by the non-linear flow of time for 2 turns. So, with 5 successes, you'd daze someone for 10 turns. The penalty is losing 3 dice from your rolls, which could do very little (11 dice to hit on a claw attack) or a lot (rolling 3 Gnosis to try and step sideways).<br /> <br /> Mages with the Time Sphere ignore this Gift entirely, which is a neat bit of Flavour, but honestly doesn't make much sense to me. A Technocrat with Time 1 has no special relationship with the flow of Time when they aren't using a Procedure to observe and measure time somehow; being able to overcome this Gift with a simple Time 1 effect I can understand, but immunity doesn't really check out. You might as well give the immunity to anyone with Chronos or Temporis too.<br /> <br /> How good is this? Well targeting WP kinda sucks at this level of play; anything worth destroying will probably have decent Willpower. Two turns per success is alright, but many Gifts last for a scene. -3 dice is pretty neat... but it depends on what kind of enemy you're facing. Arete gets utterly trashed by this, as does Gnosis, but Attribute + Ability rolls will probably be fine. Now if it halfed dice pools, now we'd be talking. Basically this is akin to giving someone a nasty painful wound, but without actually hurting them. Between the Gnosis cost, and the Willpower difficulty, and the duration measured in turns... and the simple fact that attacking will probably inflict a worse dice penalty... this isn't that great unfortunately.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shiva's Might (5):</b></u></span> Spend 2 Rage and 2 Gnosis to enter a &quot;Juddho&quot; form where you are a hyper-giant Crinos with many limbs. +6/3/6 for stats, and its flaming weapons deal Strength + 4 Aggravated (no difficulty to hit given, so presumably 6).<br /> <br /> This Gift is... decent. Costly. But decent. Being a huge combat monster is neat. But the cost is annoying, and the downside, which I won't get into too much detail on, is very annoying. Basically you lose all your Rage and fall asleep, which could easily be deleted, the moment the Gift expires.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-23-2025, 04:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-24T04:47:07-07:00","id":1549536,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Balam Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Hunter's Mists (Curse of Aeolus)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Storm of Pests (Scent of Sweet Honey)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Pathfinder</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Terrors (True Fear)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Ancestral Wings (Wings of the Pegasus)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Wandering Forest (Trackless Waste)</b></span><br /> <br /> Pathfinder arrives a rank early, and Ancestral Wings interacts with the Balam Yava; the feathers you drop from using this Gift can be used to kill you. That doesn't change the Gift's ranking, but worth keeping in mind if you don't take a Merit to overcome the Yava.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Vision Cloud (4):</b></u> Fill an area of 50 feet with a cloud of gas that forces those that breathe it to have visions. These visions are generally helpful, showing the future, or revealing lost information.<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Enigmas (difficulty 5), and on a success the Gift just... happens. To me, however, the key question is what happens if someone is trying to resist. Does holding your breath work? Presumably. How thick is the cloud? What happens if it's used in the middle of a fight? There are a lot of questions that'd need answering, and I actually kinda like the idea of a Balam stopping a fight by filling the area with vision mist that leaves everyone seeing the deaths they'll suffer if they fight one another... but that's highly ST-dependent.<br /> <br /> Ultimately this Gift could be great, or awful, and it's so much in the hands of the ST that there isn't much to say. The roll is easy, but I would just get rid of it. The cost is fine. If the Gift works as intended it might help a little, or a lot.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Feed the Gods (5):</b></u></span> Just read this!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Balam can rip out the heart of an enemy from a distance, pull it to his hand, and consume it in a burst of fire. </div> </div> </div>Sounds really awesome and powerful. Unfortunately the Gift is just a way to stunt on incredibly weak foes. Roll Gnosis, difficulty 7, and spend 2 Gnosis. If your successes are double the target's Stamina (e.g. 6 successes for Stamina 3) then their heart flies out and you can catch and burn it. Oh and also, things like Fortitude subtract 2 successes per level.<br /> <br /> This Gift needed playtesting. They were so scared of an instakill power they made it useless. But here's the thing; if you're gonna do that, don't write an instakill power! There are a lot of other ways you could write this power, for example spending more Gnosis for more successes, needing only to match the Stamina (not 2x Stamina) if the target is Mauled or worse, circumstantial difficulty reductions and so on. There could also be a benefit to the Gift successfully going off, like it strengthens the local Naturae or Gaian spirits or something like that, or your Totem grows in strength.<br /> <br /> All in all a terrible power with a really, really over the top and decently cool description.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Bubasti Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> (No pre-existing Level 1 Gifts)<br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Dreamspeak</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Barrier (Spirit Ward)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>The Many Tongues of Ptah (Speech of the World)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Arms of Darkness (Coils of the Serpent)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Shadow Brethren (Shadow Pack)</b></span><br /> <br /> Spirit Ward at Rank 2 is pretty amazing; it normally comes at Rank 4. Speech of the World is bumped up to Rank 3 from 2, annoyingly. Coils of the Serpent is also bumped up from Rank 2 to Rank 4, which still leaves the Gift as being very useful, but is pretty annoying. As a Bubasti you could very easily steal Coils of the Serpent if you ever met an Elder Brother, and it seems a relatively likely combo given the mystic inclinations. Easier said than done, don't get me wrong, but the point is that if you stole Coils of the Serpent from a Garou you might get it for 10xp at Rank 2. If you wait patiently to get it on your own list you might pay 12xp at Rank 4.<br /> <br /> All in all the Bubasti list is highly focused on esoteric and academic roles, mystic stuff too, but the base Bastet is so good at this that I'd rather play a magic-obsessed powerhouse than a Bubasti, at least personally. It's more fun in my book to play a nerd with giant muscles, and if the Bubasti list doesn't offer anything unique to help with the role of mystic, people will default to another option. Scholar's Friend is pretty handy, but also replicated by a 1-2 point Fetish almost certainly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Alms to the Poor (1):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis to create a handful of food or a few coins. This could ingratiate yourself to locals... but if you are a Bastet and you can't get your hands on some food or petty cash, I question your skills. The ability to turn 1 Gnosis into $3 or so (the book says $2, but I'm accounting for inflation) repeatedly is interesting, and in theory you could generate infinite money if you had access to infinite Gnosis, but you're never going to have access to infinite Gnosis.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scholar's Friend (1):</b></u></span> Understand anything written down in any language, so long as it isn't written in a code of some kind, in which case you can read what it <i>says</i>, but you don't necessarily understand what it <i>means</i>.<br /> <br /> For example reading some old French poetry will translate the meaning of the words. If there is a hidden message in the poem it won't be revealed, but you might spot it yourself with the right skills.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Banish Cahlash's Brood (3):</b></u></span> Evoking the authority of the Wyrm itself, based on the flavour of the Gift, you banish Banes and other Wyrmish spirits.<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Willpower, roll Manipulation + Enigmas, difficulty of either the spirit's Rage or Gnosis, whichever is <i>higher</i>. Every success removes 3 Essence. Which is nothing.<br /> <br /> In exchange your fur turns darker, and your actions get more eratic, and if you ever botch the Gift you gain a Derangement. You also smell of the Wyrm for a week after using this Gift, meaning Garou NPCs might just kill you on sight.<br /> <br /> Immediate thought is that this Gift should target whichever is lower out of Rage or Gnosis, allowing you to go after a spirit's weakness. Or perhaps you choose which to target, testing your knowledge of spirits; if you can infer from observing a spirit, or your own Occult knowledge, that a spirit has very weak Rage/Gnosis you might choose the better of the two options. Then, and this is important, the Gift needs to do something other than &quot;remove 3 essence per success&quot;. If you want to do that you have an option, though it's much more aggressive; it's called attacking!<br /> <br /> The Wyrmish spirit could lose 20% of its Essence (minimum 3) per success, for example. So say you fight a 50 Essence Wyrmish Elemental with a Gnosis of 3 and a Rage of 6. The cunning Bubasti works out that the spirit is relatively slow in its thoughts, and infers that it likely has weaker Gnosis. Manipulation + Enigmas, difficulty 3, the Bubasti gets 3 successes and the Bane loses 60% of its Essence, in this case, 30. There would be a restriction on the Gift saying you can't reuse it on the same spirit in a given month.<br /> <br /> Anyways. RAW, this Gift is absolutely terrible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-24T08:50:12-07:00","id":1549537,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ceilican Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Faerie Light</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Banish Burning (Master of Fire)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Faerie Kin</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Phantasm</b></span><br /> <b>Gift of Dreams</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>The Madness of Crowds (Riot)</b></span><br /> <br /> Note that Ceilican get Master of Fire at Rank 2, rather than Rank 1, so if you can steal it from a Homid Garou or Homid Ajaba for 5xp instead of paying 6xp, might be worth it. Phantasm, meanwhile, arrives a Rank early. The Ceilican ought to take more inspiration from the Fianna list in all honesty, but you can always try to grab those Gifts through Bastet-like mystical methods. The definitive Gift of the list is Faerie Kin, which will shine very well if your ST understands Fae.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Satyr's Wisdom (1):</b></u></span> +3 dice to any performance with a musical instrument. This might help with certain Gifts and Fetishes, but is mostly a flavour thing. That said, a truly moving performance could have a bigger impact than one might expect. I like that this is entirely passive and that it will help you to chill with the Fae.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sorcerer's Blade (2):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, make an <i>edged </i>melee weapon inflict Aggravated damage until sunrise. This is a great power to use on the weapons of allies. There is a Manipulation + Occult roll, difficulty 6, but you're very likely to pass that.<br /> <br /> The more Gnosis you have the more allies you can bless with this Gift. You can also use this for all kinds of tricksy plots. Enchanting a dueling weapon so that it will deal unexpected Aggravated might help you to frame someone for 'murdering' their kin or ally during a duel; that sort of thing. It's a real shame that this doesn't work on something like a Sap, which is difficulty 4 to hit and deals Bashing, meaning that bumping it up to Aggravated would be great.<br /> <br /> All in all this is a very neat power, and if it worked on any weapon, or any melee weapon, it might be a lot better. It'd also be great if it worked on a target's fists, fangs or so on. But you very likely can get a Fetish weapon as a PC (if only in character generation), and as a WTA PC you have innate access to Aggravated damage. This is a handy buffing power for melee characters, including allies, but the problem is that you can't buff anything beyond the change in damage type. If this Gift provided some rider effect, or a difficulty or damage adjustment, it'd be far superior. Handy, worth grabbing if you require it, but not amazing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Small Cousin (4):</b></u></span> Develop a 'housecat' form which you can transform into using the standard Transformation mechanics. I really, really wish this was a Level 1-2 Gift. Still, I like this a lot. Unlike &quot;well, my Feline form BASICALLY looks like a cat&quot;, which is a pretty silly strategy to attempt given how absolutely massive you'll be, this really lets you blend in as a normal animal.<br /> <br /> If your stats become relevant as a cat they are Strength 1, Dexterity +2, Stamina 1.<br /> <br /> Paying 12xp for this, and waiting until Rank 4, is really annoying. But I don't think it's unreasonable to say that this is a Gift you'll automatically grab the moment it becomes available. Given that this is a Level 4 Gift I could wish for your Stamina and Strength being unmodified from your Homid values, making you a freakishly strong and robust cat if you need to access such power, but still. This is a Green simply by virtue of the fact that if you're playing a Ceilican you'll want this once you can get it.<br /> <br /> Would I grab this as any other kind of Bastet? I can kinda see it. For a brutal Khan who values the power of a deadly ambush, the ability to turn into a little kitty cat is actually very cool! This Gift exists alongside options like invisibility, ignore-me powers, hypnosis, teleportation, stepping sideways and so on... but a lot of supernatural creatures who will foil or see past those won't necessarily be fooled by a cute cat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-24T09:43:38-07:00","id":1549541,"title":"","content":"Meow! Good to see the Bastet on the prowl!<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-24T12:59:05-07:00","id":1549543,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Khan Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Rhino's Favour (1):</b></u></span> This is a prominent, ugly, veil-breaking and mostly useless weapon. If your hands are out of use and you want to be able to gore with a charge, neat, but the difficulty 7 is annoying and Strength + 2 Aggravated isn't that great. Plus you need to spend Rage to access it. Gifts like this need a rider effect, like bonus dice to hit or lower difficulty when charging, to be worthwhile.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Skin of Jade (1):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Willpower, gain +2 soak. This is a great ability whether your soak is poor or excellent; going from a soak of 2 to 4 is great because you've doubled your soak, and going from a soak of 10 to 12 is great because you've significantly increased the reliability of you going &quot;nuh-uh&quot; to incoming gunfire. If you can combine this with Fetish Armour or Luna's Armour you'll get even more mileage out of it. We aren't told whether this soak works against Silver if you are in a vulnerable state, but presumably it doesn't.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Heart of Fury (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Ahroun list. This Gift is somewhat different for Khan since their Rage difficulty always defaults to 6, rather than shifting with the moon. Adding +1-3 difficulty to Rage rolls is a double-edged sword and is also very hard to pull off if you have a high Rage, which is the reason you'd want this Gift in the first place. Unfortunately the Gift still needs reworking even removed from the Garou context.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Ricepaper Walk (2):</b></u></span> Roll Gnosis, difficulty 6. For 3 turns per success you exert a weight equal to that of a scrap of paper on any surface. In theory this would let you walk on water, impossibly fragile glass, a leaf canopy and so on. If paper could sit on it, it works.<br /> <br /> This isn't a gamechanger given the Gnosis roll, which interferes with Rage, and the duration being measured in turns rather than a whole Scene. But a major downside of being a multi-tonne primordial hypercarnivore whose Chatro form makes Hispo look relatively small is that there are many, many times where your weight will lead to you breaking surfaces or causing lots of noise, setting off pressure alarms and so on. Used cleverly this Gift could be great.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Asuras' Bane (3):</b></u></span> See Banish Cahlash's Brood. This Gift will turn a weretiger's coat white instead of black, but otherwise it is identical. The Gift needs reworking and as it stands it is terrible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Paws of the Raging Spirit Tiger (3):</b></u></span> I'm absolutely shocked that there isn't a Garou Gift that does this! Swiping sideways is a hilarious idea and very fitting to their work. It's especially interesting to give this Gift to the Khan, who won't be Stepping Sideways (unless they're in the Beast Courts, or using a Merit or Fetish) until they're Rank 4. This is what made me go &quot;oh wait... can Bastet peek through the Gauntlet?&quot; And to the best of my understanding they absolutely can! That's how you aim this Gift after all.<br /> <br /> So, let's assume a Gnosis of 3-5, you roll against the local Gauntlet, you see through, and now you spot a Bane. It won't be spotting you currently, chances are it isn't peering through the Gauntlet 24/7. Spend a Gnosis, for the next 6 turns you're able to murder it through the Gauntlet with your hands/paws. I'd suggest waiting a turn, for what it's worth, so that on the very first turn you attack you can also spend Rage for bonus attacks.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that this attack isn't impeded by the Gauntlet at all, and there's no roll for the Gift's use, though of course you have to roll to attack (probably a very easy roll since your foe won't see the attack coming in most scenarios). What this means is that if you have a good way of peeking through the Gauntlet in cities, you'll be a peerless Bane-slayer, since their ability to fight back will be very limited. This is the sort of Gift which, if it had been shared between Breeds, might have led to a very different outcome in wars against the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dragonroar (4):</b></u></span> This is a powerful dragonfire attack which costs 2 Gnosis. I like that it's aimed with Dexterity + Brawl, instead of randomly expecting you to have mastered Firearms.<br /> <br /> On a hit the attack deals Ferocity <i>levels </i>of Aggravated damage. At this point, as a Rank 4 Khan, you likely have a Ferocity of 8-10. 7+ levels of Aggravated, even if it's soakable, is insane.<br /> <br /> And then it keeps going. Next turn 8 levels becomes 4, 4 becomes 2, 2 becomes 1, then it dies out. The odds of survival aren't amazing, and there's really nothing stopping you from using this Gift repeatedly beyond the Gnosis cost. This would be great even at Level 5.<br /> <br /> As a worked example, imagine a Ferocity 8 Khan uses this on my Metis PC; his default soak is 8 dice (base 5, and he is in Crinos form when Veil permits) and he has a bonus health level from Huge Size. 8 levels vs 8 dice of soak comes to an average of 4.5 levels of Aggravated damage, though in theory it could be anywhere from 0 to 8. Next turn the damage would be 4 levels vs 8 dice of soak, so an average of 0.5 levels of Aggravated, but it could be anywhere from 0-4. The 2 levels is unlikely to do anything at all, but could do 0-2 Aggravated, and finally the fire will go out with almost certainly 0 damage, but possibly 1 final level.<br /> <br /> On average this Gift wouldn't kill my boy, but a single poor soak roll, or starting in Homid, could completely ruin him. A Ferocity of 10 is also entirely achievable and makes the Gift even more deadly.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Heaven Thunder Hammer (5):</b></u> This one is very strangely worded.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The player spends three Rage and makes a Strength + Primal-Urge roll (difficulty 3) to hit every thing within 10 feet directly in front of the weretiger. The attack adds the Khan\u2019s Ferocity to its damage dice. </div> </div> </div>So you &quot;hit&quot; everything within 10 feet that's directly in front of you. You &quot;add&quot; your Ferocity to the damage dice. So does it deal Strength damage as a base? Or is the base damage your successes on the Strength + Primal-Urge roll.<br /> <br /> This is unrateable per the RAW, but let's imagine for a moment that your successes on the Strength + Primal-Urge roll, plus your Ferocity, makes up the damage dice. And that it's Aggravated damage, why not. It could easily be Bashing or Lethal, but it's a Level 5 Gift.<br /> <br /> With maxed out stats, you might expect 9-10 successes on the initial roll, and at Rank 5 your Ferocity may well be 10. So let's say it's ~20 dice of Aggravated. That, in a small directional AOE, for 3 Rage, ain't bad at all. If that <u>is</u> how it's meant to be read... then this would be a <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Green</b></span>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T02:41:38-07:00","id":1549551,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Pumonca Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> (No pre-existing Level 1 Gifts)<br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Speak With Wind Spirits</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Spirit of the Fish</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Bayou's Embrace (Quicksand)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Call Elemental</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Thunderbird's Cry (Divine Wind)</b></span><br /> <br /> Quicksand arriving a Rank early makes it slightly more useful.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mockingbird's Mirror (1):</b></u></span> Mimic any voice or sound you've heard. Entirely passive. Amazing Gift and makes you a match for a Corax in trickery.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wanderer's Boon (1):</b></u></span> Costs 1 Willpower and lets you ignore things like extreme heat, cold, starvation and thirst. Every success lets you ignore one of these things. The roll is Stamina + Survival, difficulty 6, so Crinos or Chatro helps. Repeatedly using the Gift adds to the difficulty, but the important thing is that you have a very cheap Gift to help you avoid dying from a variety of environmental risks, even if you do need to eventually access water. The durations are generous and that makes the Gift very handy.<br /> <br /> If this were a higher Level or cost Gnosis it wouldn't be Green, but this is well worth 3xp.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Thunderbolt (3):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Rage and roll Dexterity + Survival (difficulty 8 normally, 6 in a storm) to hit someone you can see with a lightning bolt. There has to be a cloud for the lightning to come from. The Gift deals (Cunning) dice of lethal damage. So at Rank 3, probably 5ish dice of damage. 5-10 dice of lethal damage simply isn't that great at Rank 3... but it's worth keeping in mind that this doesn't have to be a 'murder the serious high-level threat I can see right now' power. It can be a &quot;send a mortal to hospital&quot; power in which you don't get blamed, because who blames a weird hermit for a freak lightning strike?<br /> <br /> Given that this costs Rage and you can use it to attack weak enemies with impugnity, without them likely being able to sense where you're striking from, I'll call this Amber. If a pentex rep is assessing an oil field you can ruin their day, even if they only take a few dice of lethal damage they'd be insane not to go to the hospital.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T07:05:47-07:00","id":1549556,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Qualmi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Breakfast of Stones (Wanderer's Boon)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Turned Fur (Camouflage)</b></span><br /> (No pre-existing Level 2 Gifts)<br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Song of the Earth Mother</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Wisdom of the Ancient Ways</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Chill of Early Frost</b></span><br /> <br /> Note that Turned Fur only works on your exposed natural bodyparts; it doesn't work on clothing and gear. So if you want to increase the difficulty to find you by +3, Crinos, Chatro and Feline are your friends. Given that the Gift only costs 3xp I'd say it's well worth it... just remember that it also only works in the wilderness. Song of the Earth Mother isn't necessarily amazing but can do great work for a Qualmi, helping you find all kinds of supernatural oddities in a small region.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>No Hidden Thing (2):</b></u></span> Passive +3 Enigmas. Mostly useful for Gifts, but could help you when dealing with spirits and the Umbra. Given that a powerful Enigmas Gift is at the same Rank I'd say that this is a solid Green, for 6xp.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wind From the West (2):</b></u></span> Roll Manipulation + Enigmas, difficulty of the target's Willpower. If you succeed you completely confuse a target's sense of direction, causing them to become disorientated and intimidated. With 3+ successes you will make someone panic, and if they can Frenzy, they are likely to do so (prioritizing Fox or equivalent if available).<br /> <br /> There is no cost, and this is a great disabling power to peacefully deal with mortals and supernaturals who may interfere with your work. A pack of Garou whose Alpha suddenly runs off in a Fox frenzy may well chase after their Alpha to ensure they're okay, buying you time to finish your work or make a clean getaway. Assuming a good dice pool, and +3 dice from No Hidden Thing, you'll be passing 80%+ of the time even against someone with a Willpower of 8.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Water's Vision (5):</b></u></span> Roll Perception + Primal-Urge against the difficulty of the Gauntlet to see through everything as if it was transparent.<br /> <br /> This Gift being Level 5 for a literal Werelynx means that I am very, very disapproving of the Rank 2 xray-vision Gift Theurges get in Changing Ways. That is the only Gift from the book I would absolutely erase for any sensible game.<br /> <br /> How good is this? A distance of 100 feet per success is very neat, and the Gift could help you to discover a lot of hidden traps, assassins, fleeing prey, lost mysteries and so on. Despite the fact that this could be a Level 3/4 Gift quite easily, I'm happy to say that this is good enough to be Green. If it had a cost I'd be annoyed, but it's free to use as much as you like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T11:17:41-07:00","id":1549558,"title":"","content":"I think the Changing Breeds suffer from saturation.<br /> <br /> I love the Khan and the Simba, but rationally I feel that one feline breed was enough.<br /> <br /> With Canids was simpler, as you could go with Wolves (and some other canines) under the Werewolf umbrella. But too many subcultures for each of the Changing Breeds, and the Bastet seem to be the largest.<br /> <br /> Other companies have it simpler: one shapechanger template that has pluses or minuses on different attributes depending on meta-breed, so no siberian, husky, or german sheperd changer, just werecanine template. Same for felines, rodents, etc.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T11:42:08-07:00","id":1549559,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar4967_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4967\" data-vbnamecard=\"4967\">lbeaumanior</a> I think the Bastet work well when you think of it like a shadow of the Garou nation. Here's a bunch of tribes defined not by what Totem they follow, but by what branch of 'cat' they are kin to. If it feels like a kitchen sink, keep in mind that many of the Bastet breeds are very rare and struggling to hold onto their culture and bloodlines.<br /> <br /> The 'bloat' of Fera would be easier to manage if there were more Gift overlap and a well-edited mechanical overview (though Changing Breeds does a good job with its quick reference tables).<br /> <br /> I'd say the thing that makes me question the setting's cohesion the most is that the Ratkin are extremely numerous, outnumbering the Garou many times over. Similar thing with the Ananasi. Things start feeling crowded. But there being a huge laundry list of werebeasts who are very rare makes sense to me; if the War of Rage hadn't happened then you'd be running into all of these guys all of the time, but it did, so you don't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T17:01:51-07:00","id":1549572,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Simba Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Submit (Kneel)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Armour of Kings (Luna's Armour)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Heart of Storms (Might of Thor)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Coup de Grace</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Wrath of Kings (Wrath of Gaia)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>King of Beasts</b></span> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>(King of the Beasts)</b></span><br /> <b>Obedience</b><br /> <br /> Note that King of the Beasts (the exact name varies between W20 and W20CB) is adjusted to impact all animals within 300 feet, rather than a single animal within 100 feet; it is naturally bumped up from Rank 2 to Rank 4. Unconditional absolute power over every animal within 300 feet is potentially extremely strong, especially in Africa. Kneel is only Level 1 for Simba, instead of Level 2. For Obedience make sure to check out the Shadow Lords entry in the Garou thread.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Majesty (1):</b></u></span> +1 to Leadership and Intimidation. Pretty handy for Simba, and for only 3xp it's cheaper than buying +1 to both Talents.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sun Claws (2):</b></u></span> Spend a turn, and 1 point of Rage, activating this Gift. For the rest of the scene your fangs and claws deal +2 dice of damage, or +2 levels of damage against Vampires. A neat bonus, and worth activating if you have it and can prep before the fight with razor claws and so on.<br /> <br /> However the turn economy of the power, along with the fact that it makes it harder to regenerate (bumping the Stamina roll from difficulty 8 to difficulty 9 if you try to regenerate without resting) does make it harder to use.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Swara Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Blissful Ignorance</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Impala's Flight (Speed of Thought)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense the Unnatural</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Walking Between Worlds</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Messenger's Fortitude</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Speed Beyond Thought</b></span><br /> <br /> Note that while <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense the Unnatural</b></span> is normally Green, an argument could be made for downgrading it here. The Gift is amazing, just not necessarily on a Bastet, who get an improved version of Sense Wyrm, and Sense Magic too. Sense the Unnatural does give some budget 'Scent of the True Form' type abilities, sure, but it's not impossible for you to acquire that Gift either. All in all Sense the Unnatural remains good, but if you get a chance to buy Scent of the True Form for 5xp, Sense the Unnatural arguably becomes 100% redundant on a Bastet. Swara also uniquely get to Step Sideways at Rank 2.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Ghost Caress (4):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you send sensations to a distant target using a 'focus', another person who you touch directly. This can't truly harm the recipient but will probably upset them.<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Willpower, roll Gnosis, difficulty 7 for a short distance and 9 for for 50 miles. Whatever you do to the focus is felt by the target instead.<br /> <br /> I'm gonna say it. This power is weirdly sensual, and the write-up is all but explicitly sexual. I'm not necessarily a huge fan of that in this context. For some reason at Rank 4 Werecheetahs unlock &quot;Sex Crime: The Gift&quot;, at least the way this is written. Naturally use of this Gift could be consensual, you could give your ally a really nice back massage while they're in a meeting or tap a message to them through morse code, or play a prank on a friend where you suddenly give them the sensation of being dipped into ice water up to the neck when they aren't expecting it.<br /> <br /> You'll note that none of these feel like things a Rank 4 Fera will be doing. If this were a Level 1 Gift it might rank well, but this doesn't sit well at Rank 4. For the utility of driving a Pentex rep in a meeting crazy by repeatedly waterboarding the focus... I'll give this Amber. Just keep it PG13 people. Well, each to their own, every table is different.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>River of Blood (5):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Rage, roll Manipulation + Primal-Urge difficulty 7, or difficulty 5 if you're in a place where significant blood has been shed (recently or historically).<br /> <br /> After 3 turns blood coalesces from the soil, taking forms of your choosing. Strength and Health Levels of any structures you make with blood are equal to your successes x2, so with 5 successes you could make a constricting tentacle with a Strength of 10 and 10 Health Levels. Unfortunately since these structures don't have any other guidance on how they work, it's hard to say how this plays out. Ultimately the 3 turn delay is the real problem here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T18:37:40-07:00","id":1549573,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549482-bastet-mechanical-deep-dive?p=1549558#post1549558\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Changing Breeds suffer from saturation.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. I don't mind those breeds that clearly exist in folklore - almost every human society has a werecreature based on the local apex predator in their legends. However, some breeds are clearly more derived from high fantasy than the real world mythology and legends that the World of Darkness is based on.<br /> <br /> I'm a big believer that things not included in the original corebooks don't need to be part of any game. So I go with an ala carte approach and feel fine picking and choosing what additional elements work in my chronicles, rather than feel compelled to include everything ever published.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549482-bastet-mechanical-deep-dive?p=1549558#post1549558\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love the Khan and the Simba, but rationally I feel that one feline breed was enough.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see why there are multiple batest. Most of the big cats don't overlap except in a few places. If you are playing in North America, you really only need to include the Pumonca in your setting if mountain lions are present locally. Only if you really want to explore things like this would you expand into the Qualmi. The other Bastet don't need to be present at all.<br /> <br /> The Balam, Simba, and Khan likewise work well as the sole species in the places they are from. The Bagheera and Swara can easily be ignored unless one is running a Bastet only chronicle and you set it in India or Africa.<br /> <br /> The Bubasti and Ceilican can easily be dumped, and the setting won't lose much. These are very minor creatures that exist more for crossover and to be &quot;special&quot; than any real purpose.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549482-bastet-mechanical-deep-dive?p=1549559#post1549559\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say the thing that makes me question the setting's cohesion the most is that the Ratkin are extremely numerous, outnumbering the Garou many times over. Similar thing with the Ananasi. Things start feeling crowded.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ratkin and the Ananasi are the two breeds I think are least defensible from a perspective that the World of Darkness should be based on some kind of real world lore (or at least favorite gothic horror tropes). They seem high fantasy creatures to me. I know some players love them, but they don't do anything for me. So I don't use them in my setting at all. I do have some creatures <i>like</i> them, but they are fomori horror inspired enemy instead. And the only reason they exist is that when I was diversifying the kinds of Wyrm factions that the Garou could fight, a quick glimpse at the fomori powers already showed these archetypes were available. So after cobbling together their fomori powers, I just need to think up of some justification why these fomori exist - usually some Wyrm cult worshipping some strange Wyrm Incarna in Malfeas.<br /> <br /> I feel the same way about the Lost Breeds that perished during the War of Rage, but because they became extinct in the Stone Age that explains why modern people have no legends about them. So I am OK with these having once existed.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T21:14:28-07:00","id":1549574,"title":"","content":"Just to note, there is no such thing as &quot;Kyphur cats of Egypt&quot;. It's something Phil Brucato completely invented.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude02","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-25T22:34:58-07:00","id":1549576,"title":"","content":"Also, Ubasti/Bastet was originally presented as a lion goddess associated with the sun, possibly with Sekhmet as another side of the same figure. When she wasn't a lion, she was a domestic cat. Since domesticated anything is out for Garou and Fera, you could just have more lions around. Since the Simba got a measure of the solar associations back, it would also fit better in joining the Mokole in attacking any vampires that might have been about, and also allow us to cut out the &quot;ghoul Kyphur cats&quot; thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-26T02:43:11-07:00","id":1549578,"title":"","content":"I really don't mind the WoD just making stuff up; it's Urban Fantasy where they take a kernel of human folklore and blow it up into something mostly unique to the setting. Part of what I love about WoD is explaining things and getting responses like &quot;wait, like Cain from the Bible, that Cain&quot;, or &quot;what do you mean space isn't real&quot; or &quot;...so 9/11 brought them back?&quot;<br /> <br /> So I don't mind weresharks, wererats, were(every type of cat)s, or fictional ancient Egyptian cats which are enslaved by the Setites as Ghouled pets, or were-fae-cats which might be related to wild Scottish cats or perhaps the cats that wander the lands of the Sidhe, and I don't mind hacker Werewolves and homeless Werewolves and mutated werewolf-werewolf offspring. If you removed everything in the WoD which didn't have at least one foot in folklore, you'd lose a lot. In the context of the WoD, there are human legends in existence which don't exist IRL, and there are also supernatural factions which went undetected or who were heavily misenterpreted in history.<br /> <br /> I run into this problem when I'm talking to someone from a random country and they go &quot;well in our local folklore all vampires can do fleshcrafting-y stuff, so it makes sense to me that 100% of vampires in my country would be Tzimisce-&quot; No! Your knowledge of your local folklore is great, awesome, but I don't get the logic that human myths based on possible medieval sightings of what might have been vampires should dictate modern politics. Maybe the most famous Masquerade breach to ever happen in the country was based around a Tzimisce. Or maybe the area is like 33% Tzimisce, or was back then, so when Masquerade breaches did happen the tales tended to include warped bodies and alien forms. Or hell, maybe those weren't vampires at all.<br /> <br /> CTD has the greatest responsibility to get this stuff right, since it's ostensibly largely based on human beliefs; for everything else ordinary humans are looking at everything through a keyhole into a room full of smoke, interpreting the noises and shadows as best they can.<br /> <br /> That said, the number of Ratkin and Ananasi in existence just makes me laugh. The simple fix is to make their kinfolk exceptionally numerous but make the werebeasts themselves very rare. I don't like the idea that Nosferatu bump into Ratkin or are aware of their existence; they should be rare enough, and in big enough sewer networks, that they are realistically 100% under the radar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-26T05:01:07-07:00","id":1549580,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\">Miscellaneous mechanical details.</span><br /> <br /> By paying 3, 4 or 5 Freebie points you can free yourself of 1, 2 or 3 of your Yava via a Merit. Given that some of the Yava are utterly lethal, I think it's worth getting rid of at least those. The explanations offered are a blessing from a spirit (good plot hook) or simply that you're a 'strange prodigy' which I like less. An advantage to the 5 point version is that if you are totally free of your Yava, that means enemy won't be able to take advantage of the mystic benefits of knowing your Yava. This manifests as -2 difficulty on supernatural rolls in one direction and +2 in the other direction, which is pretty significant!<br /> <br /> For Frenzying, Bastet are much like the Garou but use a fixed difficulty of 6.<br /> <br /> Crinos inflicts Reduced Delirium, Chatro inflicts Full Delirium.<br /> <br /> Bastet get access to the Sex Appeal Merit, Cat Magic and Step Sideways Merits. Step Sideways is particularly interesting since it lets you cross the gauntlet 2-4 Ranks early depending on your circumstances (Rank 4 for most Bastet, Rank 3 for Beast Courts, Rank 2 for Swara). Cat Magic could be anything or nothing, it might let you learn Sorcery or mimic Gifts more easily or create your own Gifts/Rites with relative ease.<br /> <br /> Bastet also get access to the Secrets and Den-Realm Backgrounds, but they usually can't take Ancestors. They tend to have a personal Totem rather than a pack Totem.<br /> <br /> A Bastet's Pyrio is like a much less extreme Auspice, and it grants an additional means through which you can recover WP while slightly influencing your personality.<br /> <br /> Bastet use Cunning, Ferocity and Honour. They Rank up much like a Ragabash; you simply need to reach a certain total.<br /> <br /> Finally, the Bastet use Rites very similar to those of the Garou (though they prefer one-person Rites, such as the majority of Mystic Rites), with one noteworthy addition.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Cat Calls</b></u> allows a Bastet to put a message into a certain type of movement; say they focus on the movement of traffic, or a group of cubs frolicking, or the blooming of a flower. The information contained in this message can be up to a minute's speech, or equivalent, and any Bastet who happens to see a similar movement (traffic or cubs or flowers elsewhere in the world) will have a chance to pick up on the message by making a Wits + Enigmas, difficulty 5-8 depending on proximity. This is pretty great, especially for a Rank 1 Rite, and it helps to explain how the Bastet keep eachother updated despite being solitary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-26-2025, 08:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-26T05:10:34-07:00","id":1549581,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Final Verdict</b></u><br /> <br /> So, how good are the Bastet? Let's list pros and cons using Garou as a baseline.<br /> <br /> +++ Great Gift lists including an excellent sensory setup for finding Triat strength, magic, special items and so on<br /> +++ Forms tend to favour Dexterity, which is excellent for killing, survival and stealth; Chatro is often a very strong form<br /> ++ However the Storyteller handles Bastet 'stealing' or 'copying' Gifts, chances are it will be a significant advantage<br /> ++ Able to play a character with a high starting Rage without limiting yourself to a non-mystic role or wasting Freebies<br /> + Able to inflict Reduced Delirium if they wish, which might be helpful to minimize chaos at times<br /> + Pyrios grant an additional means of recovering Willpower<br /> + Even a 1-dot Den-Realm (a house or square mile) is a significant advantage, even once you learn to Step Sideways naturally<br /> <br /> - Yava can be a serious threat if you don't protect yourself against them with a Fetish or Merit<br /> - No Pack structure by default, though this will be overridden in a game with a core premise of a pack forming under unusual circumstances<br /> - Unable to Step Sideways unless you spend 7 freebie points or 6/8/12 xp at Rank 2/3/4 depending on your situation<br /> - Generally unable to access Ancestors","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-29T22:30:20-07:00","id":1549640,"title":"","content":"The most logical choice for the Bubasti breed is the Caracal. It is roughly the same size as the Candian Lynx or North American Bobcat, and the northern subspecies is found all around the Red Sea and Levant, as well as North Africa. (The southern species is found all across eastern and southern Africa, and the Asiatic subspecies in Iran, Pakistan, and northwest India.) And they have really cool looking distinctive ears.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-31T14:53:42-07:00","id":1549685,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549482-bastet-mechanical-deep-dive?p=1549581#post1549581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>Final Verdict</b></u><br /> <br /> So, how good are the Bastet? Let's list pros and cons using Garou as a baseline.<br /> <br /> +++ Great Gift lists including an excellent sensory setup for finding Triat strength, magic, special items and so on<br /> +++ Forms tend to favour Dexterity, which is excellent for killing, survival and stealth; Chatro is often a very strong form<br /> ++ However the Storyteller handles Bastet 'stealing' or 'copying' Gifts, chances are it will be a significant advantage<br /> ++ Able to play a character with a high starting Rage without limiting yourself to a non-mystic role or wasting Freebies<br /> + Able to inflict Reduced Delirium if they wish, which might be helpful to minimize chaos at times<br /> + Pyrios grant an additional means of recovering Willpower<br /> + Even a 1-dot Den-Realm (a house or square mile) is a significant advantage, even once you learn to Step Sideways naturally<br /> <br /> - Yava can be a serious threat if you don't protect yourself against them with a Fetish or Merit<br /> - No Pack structure by default, though this will be overridden in a game with a core premise of a pack forming under unusual circumstances<br /> - Unable to Step Sideways unless you spend 7 freebie points or 6/8/12 xp at Rank 2/3/4 depending on your situation<br /> - Generally unable to access Ancestors</div> </div> </div> </div> I was tempted to yowl at the very notion of a group of Bastet being called anything but a \u2018Pride\u2019, but given the unhappy career (aka \u2018Ramoages\u2019) of Black Tooth it might be more tactful to suggest \u2018Coalition\u2019 or \u2018Pounce\u2019 instead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-31T15:01:03-07:00","id":1549686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549482-bastet-mechanical-deep-dive?p=1549578#post1549578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really don't mind the WoD just making stuff up; it's Urban Fantasy where they take a kernel of human folklore and blow it up into something mostly unique to the setting. Part of what I love about WoD is explaining things and getting responses like &quot;wait, like Cain from the Bible, that Cain&quot;, or &quot;what do you mean space isn't real&quot; or &quot;...so 9/11 brought them back?&quot;<br /> <br /> So I don't mind weresharks, wererats, were(every type of cat)s, or fictional ancient Egyptian cats which are enslaved by the Setites as Ghouled pets, or were-fae-cats which might be related to wild Scottish cats or perhaps the cats that wander the lands of the Sidhe, and I don't mind hacker Werewolves and homeless Werewolves and mutated werewolf-werewolf offspring. If you removed everything in the WoD which didn't have at least one foot in folklore, you'd lose a lot. In the context of the WoD, there are human legends in existence which don't exist IRL, and there are also supernatural factions which went undetected or who were heavily misenterpreted in history.<br /> <br /> I run into this problem when I'm talking to someone from a random country and they go &quot;well in our local folklore all vampires can do fleshcrafting-y stuff, so it makes sense to me that 100% of vampires in my country would be Tzimisce-&quot; No! Your knowledge of your local folklore is great, awesome, but I don't get the logic that human myths based on possible medieval sightings of what might have been vampires should dictate modern politics. Maybe the most famous Masquerade breach to ever happen in the country was based around a Tzimisce. Or maybe the area is like 33% Tzimisce, or was back then, so when Masquerade breaches did happen the tales tended to include warped bodies and alien forms. Or hell, maybe those weren't vampires at all.<br /> <br /> CTD has the greatest responsibility to get this stuff right, since it's ostensibly largely based on human beliefs; for everything else ordinary humans are looking at everything through a keyhole into a room full of smoke, interpreting the noises and shadows as best they can.<br /> <br /> That said, the number of Ratkin and Ananasi in existence just makes me laugh. The simple fix is to make their kinfolk exceptionally numerous but make the werebeasts themselves very rare. I don't like the idea that Nosferatu bump into Ratkin or are aware of their existence; they should be rare enough, and in big enough sewer networks, that they are realistically 100% under the radar.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that Ratkin tend to be bonkers and fairly aggressive (and Nosferatu are very sneaky AND blessed with an animal control discipline), so it\u2019s not impossible that while they\u2019re not generally on speaking terms, there have been at least a few cases where the rats were glimpsed and possibly a little more than glimpsed.<br /> <br /> On the other hand I do agree that Real World mythology (and to some degree Real World history) are useful references but not invariably reliable guides - with their exact degree of accuracy varying over time and from case to case.<br /> <br /> As you mention, a source that very reliably describes one vampire might be much less reliable when taken to be a universal descriptor of the type.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}